 "music": "bed",
 "title": "You press Enter. Then what?",
 "sources": [
  "MDN, 'Populating the page: how browsers work' (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Performance/Guides/How_browsers_work, fetched 2026-11-24): DNS lookup returns an IP address which 'will likely be cached'; TCP three-way handshake 'SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK'; 'a new TCP connection and a full TLS handshake take two round trips (TLS 1.3) or three (TLS 1.2) before the browser can make the request'; then the browser 'sends an initial HTTP GET request'.",
  "The catch (round trips cost more time over longer distances) is a plain consequence of the steps above, not a quote."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "You type a site and press Enter. How many round trips before your browser can even ask for the page? Guess.",
   "caps": "YOU PRESS *ENTER | HOW MANY *ROUND TRIPS? | *GUESS"
  },
  {
   "text": "First, DNS turns the name into an IP address, unless it's already cached.",
   "caps": "*DNS | NAME TO *IP | OR *CACHED"
  },
  {
   "text": "Then a TCP handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK. That's one round trip.",
   "caps": "*TCP HANDSHAKE | SYN, SYN-ACK, *ACK | *ONE ROUND TRIP"
  },
  {
   "text": "Then a TLS handshake to agree on keys. With TLS one point three, that's one more.",
   "caps": "*TLS HANDSHAKE | TLS *1.3 | *ONE MORE"
  },
  {
   "text": "So that's two round trips after DNS, or three with older TLS one point two.",
   "caps": "*TWO ROUND TRIPS | *THREE ON TLS 1.2"
  },
  {
   "text": "Only then does the browser send its GET request, and the HTML starts coming back.",
   "caps": "THEN *GET | *HTML ARRIVES"
  },
  {
   "text": "The catch: every round trip costs time, so a far away server feels slow before it sends a single byte.",
   "caps": "THE *CATCH | *DISTANCE COSTS | BEFORE A *BYTE"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's why nearby servers matter so much. Did you guess two?",
   "caps": "*NEARBY SERVERS | DID YOU GUESS *TWO?"
  }
 ],
